# Arrays

Arrays are specified by first declaring their axis lengths and then their data type as in `[2][3]float32` which defines an array of `float32` with 2 axes of lengths 2 and 3. The content of the array can be defined using the `{}` notation.

In an array literal, `[...]` lets the compiler infer the axis lengths from the content of the literal. A single `[...]` stands for all the axes: `[...]int{{1, 2, 3}, {4, 5, 6}}` has 2 rows of 3 elements, so its type is `[2][3]int`. All the rows must have the same length. Axis lengths are also checked by the compiler. For example, the following code does not compile:

```
package main

func Main() [2][3]float32 {
    return [3][2]float32{{1, 2}, {3, 4}, {5, 6}} // ERROR cannot use [3][2]float32 as [2][3]float32
}
```

Arrays of `string` are not supported.

```code:options
{ "chapter_title": "Arrays" }
```

